  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-rademacher-complexity": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero."
    ],
    "thm-rademacher-contraction-inequality": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $m\\ge1$, let $z_1,\\ldots,z_m$ be fixed points in a set $\\mathcal Z$, and let $\\mathcal H$ be a nonempty class of functions $h:\\mathcal Z\\to\\mathbb R$. For each $i$, let $\\Phi_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be $L$-Lipschitz for one common $L\\ge0$. Suppose either that $\\mathcal H$ is finite or countable, or that it has a specified nonempty countable subclass $\\mathcal H_0$ for which, for every sign vector $\\sigma\\in\\{-1,+1\\}^m$, both displayed suprema equal the corresponding suprema over $\\mathcal H_0$. Assume the suprema are finite. With independent uniform signs $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ and the one-sided normalization\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal H):=\\frac1m\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{h\\in\\mathcal H}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i h(z_i),$$\n\none has\n\n$$\\frac1m\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{h\\in\\mathcal H}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i\\Phi_i(h(z_i))\\le L\\,\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal H).$$\n\nNo condition $\\Phi_i(0)=0$ is required."
    ],
    "thm-rademacher-symmetrization-bound": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Let $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ be IID with law $P$, let $S'=(Z_1',\\ldots,Z_m')$ be an independent IID copy on a finite product probability extension, and let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs independent of both samples. Write $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $P_m'g=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i')$. Assume the random suprema $\\sup_g(Pg-P_mg)$, $\\sup_g(P_m'g-P_mg)$, $\\sup_g\\sum_i\\sigma_i(g(Z_i')-g(Z_i))$, and $\\sup_g\\sum_i\\sigma_i g(Z_i)$ are measurable on their respective product sigma algebras. Then, with $\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)$ the expected one-sided Rademacher complexity,\n\n$$\\mathbb E_S\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}(Pg-P_mg)\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThis is a one-sided expectation inequality; it does not assert a tail bound or an absolute-value deviation bound. No choice axiom is assumed."
    ],
    "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem."
    ],
    "cor-erm-excess-risk-from-uniform-deviation": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume AC. Let $(\\mathcal X,\\Sigma_X)$, $(\\mathcal Y,\\Sigma_Y)$, and $(\\mathcal A,\\Sigma_A)$ be nonempty measurable spaces, let $P$ be a probability measure on $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, let $\\mathcal H$ be a nonempty class of measurable predictors $h:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal A$, and let $\\ell:\\mathcal A\\times\\mathcal Y\\to[0,1]$ be measurable. For $h\\in\\mathcal H$, define $g_h(x,y):=\\ell(h(x),y)$, $\\mathcal G:=\\{g_h:h\\in\\mathcal H\\}$, and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy, and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ required by the uniform generalization theorem are measurable in their respective product sigma algebras. Let $Z_i=(X_i,Y_i)$ be mutually independent with common law $P$, with $m\\ge1$, and write\n\n$$R_P(h):=Pg_h,\\qquad \\widehat R_S(h):=P_mg_h.$$\n\nSuppose a measurable prediction rule $\\widehat h_\\eta$ exists such that each samplewise output is an $\\eta$-approximate empirical risk minimizer, meaning $\\widehat R_S(\\widehat h_\\eta(S))\\le\\inf_{h\\in\\mathcal H}\\widehat R_S(h)+\\eta$ for a fixed finite $\\eta\\ge0$. Assume $S\\mapsto R_P(\\widehat h_\\eta(S))$ is measurable. For $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, with independent uniform signs $\\sigma_i$, $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sigma_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)=\\mathbb E_S\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)$, each of the following excess-risk bounds holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$:\n\n$$R_P(\\widehat h_\\eta(S))-\\inf_{h\\in\\mathcal H}R_P(h)\\le4\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+2\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}}+\\eta,$$\n\n$$R_P(\\widehat h_\\eta(S))-\\inf_{h\\in\\mathcal H}R_P(h)\\le4\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+6\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}+\\eta.$$\n\nThe two displayed bounds are separate probability guarantees. This corollary asserts no existence of an empirical-risk-minimizing measurable rule for a general class."
    ],
